Appendix C: Evolutionary System Identification (ESI)

C.1 Problem Statement

In a living biosphere, the dynamics \mathbf{f}(\cdot) evolve. We model this as a slowly time‑varying parameter vector \boldsymbol{\theta}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^p:

\dot{\mathbf{x}} = \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{u}; \boldsymbol{\theta}(t)) + \mathbf{w}.

The true function \mathbf{f} and the parameter evolution law \dot{\boldsymbol{\theta}} = \mathbf{g}(\boldsymbol{\theta}, \mathbf{x}) are unknown. The goal is to maintain an online estimate \hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(t) and a reliable predictive model.

C.2 Adaptive Observer with Forgetting Factor

We employ a library of candidate basis functions \boldsymbol{\phi}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{u}) \in \mathbb{R}^{p} and assume that \mathbf{f} can be approximated by a sparse linear combination:

\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{u}; \boldsymbol{\theta}) \approx \boldsymbol{\Phi}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{u}) \boldsymbol{\theta},

where \boldsymbol{\Phi} is a matrix of basis evaluations. The parameter estimate is updated via recursive least squares with exponential forgetting:

\begin{aligned}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}_{k+1} &= \hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}_k + \mathbf{L}_k (\mathbf{x}_{k+1} - \hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k+1}), \\
\mathbf{L}_k &= \mathbf{P}_k \boldsymbol{\phi}_k (\lambda + \boldsymbol{\phi}_k^T \mathbf{P}_k \boldsymbol{\phi}_k)^{-1}, \\
\mathbf{P}_{k+1} &= \lambda^{-1} (\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{L}_k \boldsymbol{\phi}_k^T) \mathbf{P}_k.
\end{aligned}

Here \lambda \in (0,1] is the forgetting factor. The estimation error \tilde{\boldsymbol{\theta}} = \hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}} - \boldsymbol{\theta} satisfies a linear time‑varying difference equation. Using standard adaptive control theory, if the true parameters vary slowly enough (\|\dot{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\| \le \delta_\theta), the error remains uniformly ultimately bounded.

C.3 Sparse Identification of Nonlinear Dynamics (SINDy) in a Closed Loop

The offline discovery of governing equations uses SINDy. Given a trajectory, we build a library \boldsymbol{\Theta}(\mathbf{X}) and solve

\dot{\mathbf{X}} = \boldsymbol{\Theta}(\mathbf{X}) \boldsymbol{\Xi},

for a sparse coefficient matrix \boldsymbol{\Xi} via sequentially thresholded least squares. In a closed biosphere, SINDy is re‑run on sliding windows. A change‑point detection algorithm on the coefficient pattern triggers re‑certification of stability margins. This is the computational implementation of ESI.

