Appendix D: Ecological Computation Theory — Formal Framework

D.1 Definition of Ecological Computation

An artificial biosphere is a distributed computing system. The total computational capacity \mathcal{C}_{\text{eco}} is the sum of information processing rates across all biological and technological nodes.

· Metabolic computation: A microbial cell processes environmental signals via regulatory networks. The mutual information between input signal S and output response R is I(S;R). Over N cells, the aggregated processing rate is \Phi_{\text{bio}} = \sum_i I(S_i;R_i) / \tau_i, where \tau_i is the characteristic response time.
· Ecological memory: The state of a soil microbiome encodes a memory of past environmental conditions. The memory capacity can be quantified by the mutual information between past perturbation type and current community structure: M = I(\text{Perturbation}; \mathbf{p}_{\text{current}}).
· Plant–fungal networks: Mycorrhizal networks transmit stress signals. The channel capacity (in bits per second) of such networks is a subject of ongoing research, but initial bounds can be derived from signal propagation speed and branching architecture.

D.2 Resilience as Computational Redundancy

A diverse ecosystem can be viewed as an error‑correcting code. The loss of a species is analogous to a bit flip. Functional redundancy ensures that the “message” (overall ecosystem function) is preserved. We adapt the Hamming bound: an ecosystem can tolerate t species extinctions without functional loss if its functional diversity satisfies

\sum_{i=0}^{t} \binom{S}{i} \le 2^{F},

where S is the number of species and F is the number of distinct functional groups encoded. This provides a lower bound on the computational redundancy inherent in biodiversity.

