Appendix E: Algorithmic Anti‑Fragility and Survival Constraints

E.1 Formal Definition of the Anti‑Fragility Objective

The Anti‑Fragility objective function is

J_{\text{AF}} = \mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{w}} \left[ \sum_{j} w_j \mathcal{D}_j(\mathbf{x}(T)) - \lambda \int_0^T \|\mathbf{u}(t)\|^2 dt \right],

where \mathcal{D}_j are biodiversity and social‑cohesion metrics, w_j are weights, and the expectation is over disturbance realisations. The control input energy penalty discourages brittle over‑intervention.

E.2 Chance‑Constrained Survival Manifold

The hard constraint \mathbf{x}(t) \in \Omega_{\text{civilisation}} \forall t is implemented as a chance constraint:

\mathbb{P}\left( \min_{t \in [0,T]} \psi(\mathbf{x}(t)) \ge 0 \right) \ge 1 - \alpha,

where \psi(\cdot) is a scalar function negative outside the survival manifold. Using a conservative deterministic surrogate (e.g., via scenario optimisation or a robust tube MPC), one can guarantee that the true probability bound holds.

E.3 Proof of Bounded Optimality

Maximising J_{\text{AF}} subject to the chance constraint yields a Pareto‑optimal trade‑off between resilience and efficiency. A Lyapunov‑based proof shows that for a sufficiently large weight on diversity metrics, the optimal control avoids the boundary of \Omega_{\text{civilisation}}, thus preventing the catastrophic optimiser trap.

