Appendix F: Biosphere Constitutional Theory — Temporal Logic Verification

F.1 Formalising Invariants

We use Linear Temporal Logic (LTL) to encode constitutional invariants. Examples:

· Oxygen safety: \square (pO_2 \ge 19.5 \, \text{kPa} \land pO_2 \le 23.5 \, \text{kPa}).
· Genetic library preservation: \square (\text{richness}_{\text{core}} \ge \mathcal{D}_{\min}).
· Amendment procedure: A designated governance agent may issue an amend action only if a supermajority of a verification oracle validates the change. This is expressed as a constrained liveness property.

F.2 Model Checking the Biosphere Constitution

Given a finite‑state abstraction of the biosphere’s high‑level state transitions (constructed via \delta-decidability or a discrete‑event model), a model checker (e.g., NuSMV) can verify that the constitutional LTL specifications hold under all admissible control actions. If a counterexample is found, the constitution is revised before deployment. This ensures that the biosphere cannot be driven into an unconstitutional state by any combination of automated decisions.

