Appendix G: Civilisational Risk Theory — Extinction Probability Bounds

G.1 Cascade Failure Model

The biosphere is a network of N interdependent subsystems (oxygen, water, food, social). Each subsystem i has a binary state: healthy (1) or failed (0). Failures propagate according to a set of interdependent thresholds. The system state is a Markov chain with an absorbing “total collapse” state.

G.2 Survival Probability over Horizon T

The generator matrix \mathbf{Q} for the non‑absorbing states is extracted from failure and repair rates. The survival function is

R(t) = \mathbf{p}_0 \exp(\mathbf{Q} t) \mathbf{1}.

If the dominant eigenvalue of \mathbf{Q} is -\lambda_{\min}, then for large t, R(t) \approx c e^{-\lambda_{\min} t}. The mean time to failure is \text{MTTF} = 1/\lambda_{\min}. For a design horizon T, the survival probability is

\mathbb{P}(\text{survival}) \approx e^{-\lambda_{\min} T}.

We set the civilisational stability criterion as \mathbb{P}(\text{survival}) \ge 0.999 over 200 years, which translates to a required \lambda_{\min} \le 5 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{yr}^{-1}.

G.3 Extreme Value Analysis for Black Swans

For each subsystem, we model extreme perturbations using the Generalised Pareto Distribution (GPD) fitted to tail data. The probability of a perturbation exceeding the subsystem’s designed safety margin is computed. The union bound over all subsystems gives a conservative upper bound on the system‑wide catastrophe probability per year. This informs the required safety margins.

